two address shapes pass2 has no rule for, dropped silently
==========================================================

Both emit an XXXXXX marker into the assembler output, produce an object
file, and exit 0.  The statement is simply absent from the code.

	1.  a literal used as an address

		v = *(int *)0x50;		BAD
		v = ((int *)0x50)[0];		BAD
		v = *(int *)0;			BAD
		v = *(int *)(0x40+0x10);	BAD
		int *p = (int *)0x50; v = *p;	ok
		int i; i = 0x50; v = *(int *)i;	ok

	    Any constant.  A pointer variable or an int variable holding
	    the same value is fine, which is the workaround.

	2.  subtraction from an address

		e = buf - 1;			BAD
		e = &buf[15] - 1;		BAD
		e = buf + 15 - 1;		BAD
		e = &buf[15] + 1;		ok
		e = &buf[14];			ok

	    Addition folds to the symbol-plus-offset shape pass2 wants.
	    Subtraction does not.

The two marker texts are not two bugs.  XXXXXX incomplete is what an
expression produces and XXXXXX unreduced condition is what the same
thing produces inside a condition:

	if (*(char *)0x50 == 1)		unreduced condition
	v = *(char *)0x50;		incomplete

so the wording says where it surfaced, not what is wrong.

What follows is how the first one was found.


a store through a cast constant is dropped, silently
====================================================

	*(int *)0x50 = &cmd;

compiles to nothing.  Not to wrong code - to no code at all.  cc exits
0, the object file is produced, and the assignment is absent from it.

This was found the long way round.  The hard disk's second level boot
sets the controller's channel command address and then kicks it:

	*(int *)HDC_CCA = &cmd;		/* 0x50 */
	*(char *)(HDC_CCA + 2) = 0;
	out(HDC_RESET, 0);

The controller went on using the pointer the rom had left there,
re-executed the previous command block, never wrote a status byte, and
the loader spun on it forever.  What that looked like from the outside
was a boot that printed its banner and stopped.  Two hours went into the
disk, the geometry and the rotation before anyone looked at the
assembler output, because everything upstream of it was plausible.


what it does
------------

Every form of it, both directions, byte and word:

	int cmd, v;
	f()
	{
	    *(int *)0x50 = &cmd;	/* store a pointer */
	    *(char *)0x52 = 0;		/* store a byte */
	    *(int *)0x54 = v;		/* store a variable */
	    v = *(int *)0x56;		/* and a load */
	}

	$ ccc -s -o cb.s cb.c ; echo $?
	0
	$ grep -c XXXXXX cb.s
	4

	; XXXXXX incomplete: ; (ASSIGN:short#1->HL (DEREF:short#1->HL
	;     80:short/v#1->HL) (SYMREFptr _cmd+0#1->HL))
	; XXXXXX incomplete: ; (ASSIGN:byte#1->HL (DEREF:byte#1->HL
	;     82:short/v#1->HL) 0:ubyte/v#1->HL)
	; XXXXXX incomplete: ; 84:short/v#1->HL
	; XXXXXX incomplete: ; (ASSIGN:short#1->HL (SYMREFptr _v+0#1)
	;     (DEREF:short/v#1->HL 86:short/v#1->HL))

So the missing rule is DEREF of a constant - an address that is a
literal rather than a symbol or a variable.  pass2 has nothing that
matches the shape, prints the marker, and moves on.


the marker is the real bug
--------------------------

The missing rule is a gap and gaps happen.  What makes this one
expensive is that it does not fail the build.  XXXXXX is a comment in
the assembler output; nothing counts them, nothing exits non-zero, and
the program that comes out the other end is one statement shorter than
the one that went in.

A compiler is allowed to not implement something.  It is not allowed to
say it did.  Anything that emits XXXXXX should fail the compilation, or
at the very least return non-zero, so that a build stops rather than
shipping a binary with a hole in it.

That would have turned this into a two minute fix.


the workaround
--------------

Go through a pointer variable, which is a shape pass2 does have:

	int *cca = (int *)0x50;
	*cca = &cmd;

	ld hl,(_cca)
	ld (hl),e
	ld (hl),d		/* and no markers */

It costs two bytes and an indirection, and it is what mwio.c does now.


where else this bites
---------------------

The shape is the natural way to talk to fixed hardware addresses, so it
is exactly what a driver or a bootstrap reaches for:

	micronix/stand/mwio.c:91	*(char *)0x84 = 0xff;
	micronix/stand/mwio.c:95	if (*(char *)0x84 == i)
	micronix/sys/main.c:168		#define ADDR (*(char*)0x1000)

The kernel one is a macro, so every use of it is affected.  None of
these would have reported anything.

